# History, activity and statistics

History answers what entities reported over a chosen period. Activity records events; statistics aggregate supported numeric observations. None of these is automatically covered by the maintained Energy chart adapter, even when the surrounding theme colours match.

**HA-CTRL-011 — reference design specification.** This specifies the local design coverage requested by HA-GAP-011 for an existing Home Assistant capability. The examples do not install a native adapter, connect to a backend or operate equipment.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-011-capabilities">Task and capability contract</h2>

| Surface | Data and interaction contract |
| --- | --- |
| History panel | Preserve selected targets, filters, period, native zoom and available export. Recorder determines stored history. |
| History graph | Keep state transitions and numeric observations in their native units; do not turn categorical states into a smoothed numeric line. |
| Activity | An event record is not a continuous measurement or proof of a physical action's completion. Preserve event time and actual attribution. |
| Statistics graph | Identify the chosen aggregate and interval: mean, minimum, maximum or supported cumulative/difference measure are not interchangeable. |
| Download or data equivalent | State the actual selected scope, dates, units and missing-data meaning. Do not offer a fake export. |

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-011-states">States, pending work and failure</h2>

Differentiate no selected targets, a successful empty period, an entity excluded from Recorder, unavailable source data and a failed request. Do not convert these into a flat zero series. A recent entity may have no earlier records; a shorter retention period can remove raw history while longer-term statistics remain.

When a person changes the date, retain the previous period label with any retained plot until the new response is ready. Do not show new-period headings over old-period values. A failed request should keep the selected scope available for review, while labelling any retained values with their original period.

Keep gaps, signed values and unit distinctions intact. A graph of mean temperature is not a list of every original reading. If comparing periods, name both periods and coverage before calculating a change. Never infer that a missing activity attribution means a physical button was pressed.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-011-content">Content and interpretation</h2>

Give each output its quantity, selected period and source. Describe timestamps as observations, events or interval boundaries according to the actual model. Use the person's native date, number and zone preferences for dynamic content. A fixed reference table includes its date and zone so it remains understandable after publication.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-011-accessibility">Accessibility and interaction</h2>

Test the native keyboard and non-visual routes on the installed frontend. A hover tooltip alone is insufficient for exact values. Use the native export or an explicitly implemented equivalent when available; do not promise the Energy View data table here. Record unresolved chart-access issues with a specific reproduction rather than declaring a themed chart accessible. Keep zoom and source controls named and usable at narrow widths.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-011-integration">Native integration boundary</h2>

The companion does not target History hosts. Preserve native History, Activity and statistics implementation and supported theme variables. The HTML tables below are design references, not a data-fetching adapter. Before a future adapter is accepted, document the source/aggregation contract, ownership of period changes and teardown, and the exact accessible alternative it adds.

